#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""image-bump -- move a service's pinned container image, or an image build's pinned inputs, to a
newer release, with a gate.

WHY THIS EXISTS
===============
A service run from a container image is pinned by DIGEST, not by tag: a tag can be re-pointed
at different bytes, a digest cannot. A pin is a single line in a file the service's unit reads:

    <image>:<tag>@sha256:<64 hex digits>

Moving it is a procedure that gets repeated for every release: resolve the new tag to the
digest of one platform, scan the image, decide whether the scan allows the move, and later
install the new pin and check that the service came back, rolling back if it did not. Done by
hand each time, a step gets skipped or done differently. This tool is that procedure, written
once. It knows the procedure and offers a few named strategies; a DESCRIPTOR beside each
service selects among them and supplies the values.

VERBS
=====
  image-bump check    <service> <version> [--no-write] [--root DIR]
                                           resolve, scan, decide; writes a scan record
                                           (and the pin: see ARTIFACTS for pinned inputs)
  image-bump deploy   <service> [--root DIR]   install the checked pin, run the checks, roll back
  image-bump latest   <service> [--root DIR] [--pin VERSION]
                                           is a newer release out than the pin
  image-bump validate <service> [--root DIR]   load the descriptor and refuse a malformed one

LATEST
======
`latest` asks the release host (`release_api.host`: Codeberg or GitHub) whether a release newer
than the pinned version exists; it never reads the registry. The current version is the pin
file's tag with `tag_suffix` and a leading "v" removed, or --pin VERSION when given (so a live
run can pretend the pin is older and see an alert; nothing is ever written). Versions compare as
tuples of integers. A tag that does not map back through `git_tag` to a purely numeric dotted
version (`v15.0.12-rc1`, `nightly`) is skipped, never compared.

  patch-of-pinned-major  GET <api>/repos/<repo>/releases (limit=50 on Codeberg, per_page=50 on
                         GitHub); drops drafts and pre-releases; the answer is the newest
                         release with the pinned major and a greater version.
  github-latest-stable   GET <api>/repos/<repo>/releases/latest (the host leaves pre-releases
                         out); the answer is its tag when newer than the pin, any major.

It prints `newer <service> <current> -> <newest> (<git tag>)` and exits 7, or
`up to date <service> <current>` and exits 0. A transport failure, an HTTP error, a body that
is not JSON or not the expected shape, or a tag it cannot read is exit 2 with a one-line reason:
failing to read is never "up to date".

DEPLOY
======
`deploy` runs on the container host, after the pin file has been applied there. It reads the
applied pin and the scan record for that digest (the one in <scan_dir> whose `digest` header
equals it), reads the running image's digest from the container (`podman inspect <container>
--format {{.ImageName}}`), and refuses (4), each with its own message, unless:

  - the applied digest differs from the running one (else: pin not applied or already running);
  - the running digest is the record's `replaces`;
  - `podman image exists <image>@<replaces>` succeeds, since the rollback needs that image;
  - the applied digest is not listed in <state>/image-bump/<service>.rolled-back, where <state>
    is XDG_STATE_HOME or ~/.local/state.

Then, in order: when the record says `migrates: yes`, stop the service in the VM and export the
volume to ~/<volume>-pre-<version>.tar; keep the VM's unit as <vm_unit>.pre-<version>; install
(`vm-copy`: copy the applied unit into the VM and reload systemd; `script`: run the script, whose
non-zero exit is a failed check); for `vm-copy` restart the service; run every descriptor check
(a command, no shell; a pass is exit 0), retrying for up to --wait seconds (default 60).

A failure with no migration restores the kept unit, reloads, restarts, re-runs the checks, lists
the applied digest as rolled back and exits 5: the pin must then be reverted by a commit. A
failure on a migrating version restores nothing and exits 6, naming the export. Success exits 0
and prints the reminders.

CHECK
=====
`check` turns <version> into a tag (the pinned tag with its version part replaced), resolves it
with `skopeo inspect --raw` to the manifest digest of the descriptor's platform (refusing an
image whose configuration is for another platform), then scans that digest and the current pin's
digest with trivy (the vulnerability database is fetched once, then both scans run with
--skip-db-update so both see the same database). The descriptor's gate rule decides:

  fixable-count-floor  no fixable HIGH/CRITICAL finding: pass; fewer than the current pin
                       carries: exception (3); otherwise refused (4). Unfixed findings are
                       recorded, not counted. A first pin (no architecture's pins line has
                       a URL and hash yet) with fixable findings: exception (3), its findings in
                       the scan record.
  no-new-findings      pass when every (vulnerability id, package) of the new image is already
                       in the current pin's findings, fixed or not; otherwise refused (4).

It then asks the descriptor's migration detector whether the version changes the data schema
(`added-files`: the directory listing of the descriptor's path at the old and at the new git tag,
read from the release host's contents API; migrating when the new listing names a file the old
lacks. An unreadable listing is an environment error, never "no migration"; `always`). Unless refused or run with --no-write it writes a
scan record <scan_dir>/<image name>-<version>-<date>.txt (a title line, `key: value` header lines,
a blank line, the findings tables; `deploy` reads `replaces`, `migrates` and `digest` from it
through read_record) and rewrites the pin file. An existing record is never overwritten.
The release API bases are overridable with IMAGE_BUMP_CODEBERG_API and IMAGE_BUMP_GITHUB_API.

ARTIFACTS
=========
An image build keeps a pinned-inputs file per architecture (one input per line, fields separated
by spaces). Besides container images it holds two kinds of line this tool can move:

    file <path> <https URL> sha256:<hex>     a binary fetched from the URL, installed at <path>
    deb <name> <https URL> sha256:<hex>      a Debian package from outside Debian

A descriptor with an `artifact` field is an ARTIFACT descriptor. It names both architectures and
both pins files, and `check` moves both together: it resolves, verifies, downloads and scans the
artifact for every architecture BEFORE it writes either pins file, so the files never disagree on a
version and one architecture failing leaves both untouched. There is no `deploy`: the next image
build bakes the pin in, so `deploy` refuses an artifact descriptor (4). `latest` works as for a
container, reading the current version from the arm pins line's URL (the first architecture, in
name order, when none is called arm). `check` and `latest` refuse (4) pins files that name
different versions; `validate`, `check` and `latest` refuse (4) a pins file with no line, or more
than one, for the artifact.

  artifact    dict  {"kind": "release-file", "path": "<absolute install path>",
                     "asset": "<name>", "sbom": "<name>"}  or
                    {"kind": "release-deb", "package": "<Debian package name>", "asset": "<name>"}
                    `asset` (and the optional `sbom`) must hold both {version} and {arch}; the
                    file name under which a release publishes the artifact for one architecture.
                    The pins line it owns is `file <path>` or `deb <package>`.
  arches      dict  {"arm": "arm64", "x86": "amd64"}: pins-file key -> the spelling of {arch}
  pins_files  dict  the same keys -> the pins file. A relative path is taken from the CURRENT
                    DIRECTORY, because the file belongs to the repository the tool is run from
                    (descriptors live in that repository, under the --root directory); `scan_dir`
                    is read the same way. Only `verify.key_file` is relative to the descriptor.
  verify      dict  how the hash is trusted, one of:
                    {"kind": "cosign-bundle", "checksums": "<name>", "bundle": "<name>",
                     "certificate_identity": "<identity>", "certificate_oidc_issuer": "<url>"}
                    {"kind": "apt-release", "repo": "<https URL>", "suite": "<suite>",
                     "component": "<component>", "fingerprint": "<40 hex>",
                     "key_file": "<public key file>"}
                    `release-file` takes `cosign-bundle`, `release-deb` takes `apt-release`.
  release_line, release_api, git_tag, scan_dir, gate, checklist
                    as for a container (`latest` and the scan record use them); `gate` is
                    fixable-count-floor or no-new-findings. None of the container fields
                    (image, platform, pin_file, migrations, volume, container, service, vm_unit,
                    install, checks, tag_suffix) may be present.

cosign-bundle  The release's checksums file and the sigstore bundle over it are fetched from
               <github>/<repo>/releases/download/<git tag>/ and checked with
               `cosign verify-blob --bundle <bundle> --certificate-identity <identity>
               --certificate-oidc-issuer <issuer> <checksums>`; {version} in `asset`, `sbom`,
               `checksums`, `bundle` and `certificate_identity` is replaced. A non-zero status is a
               refusal (4), and nothing else is fetched first. Each asset's hash is then taken only
               from that verified file (a name it lacks, or lists with two hashes, is refused),
               the asset is downloaded, and bytes that do not hash to it are refused. The pin is
               the release URL and that hash. The `sbom` asset, when named, is fetched and its
               hash recorded; it is not in the signed file, and the record says so. <github> is
               https://github.com, or IMAGE_BUMP_GITHUB_DOWNLOAD.
apt-release    `fingerprint` is the full fingerprint of the one key that signs the suite. The key
               file must hold exactly one primary key whose fingerprint is that one (else refused:
               a key is trusted because its fingerprint is pinned, never because it is in a
               file); it is imported into a throwaway gpg home and exported as a keyring holding
               only it. <repo>/dists/<suite>/Release is checked with `gpgv` against Release.gpg,
               or the clearsigned InRelease when there is no Release.gpg, and must be signed by a
               key whose primary fingerprint is the pinned one (an expired key fails). The
               Release's SHA256 section gives the hash of <component>/binary-<arch>/Packages
               (or .gz, .xz, in that order of preference); the index is fetched and refused unless
               it matches that hash and size. The package's entry is the one with Package,
               Architecture and a Filename whose last component is `asset`, exactly one; its
               SHA256 and Filename give the pin (<repo>/<Filename>), and the downloaded .deb is
               refused unless it hashes to that SHA256.

Scanning. Each architecture's artifact is laid out as a root filesystem (a file at its install
path; a .deb unpacked, by this tool, regular files only) and scanned with `trivy rootfs`; the
findings of both architectures are merged, and the current pin's artifacts (downloaded from the
pinned URLs and refused if they no longer match their pinned hash) are scanned the same way for
the gate to compare. A pin that is still PLACEHOLDER has no findings to compare, so a first pin
with fixable findings is an exception (3) under fixable-count-floor, not a refusal. When trivy
detects NO PACKAGE in an architecture's artifact (a binary with no readable dependency list), the
scan says nothing about it: that is printed as NO COVERAGE, recorded as `coverage: none`, and the
verdict becomes an exception (3) however clean the findings look, never a pass. A refusal by the
gate stays a refusal.

The scan record <scan_dir>/<name>-<version>-<date>.txt (`<name>` is the install path's last
component, or the package) has a title line, then header lines: artifact, version, `verify` (what
signed what), one line per architecture (URL and hash), `sbom-<arch>` lines, replaces (the
version it replaces or none), scanner, database, scanned, gate, verdict, packages (how many
trivy detected per architecture), coverage (yes or none); then the findings tables. The pins
line is rewritten in place: only the URL and hash change, a trailing comment stays, and no other
line is touched. A pinned URL must be https (http only to the loopback, for tests). A download
that ends before the length the server announced is an environment error (2), not a refusal.
`check` also refuses a version that is not dotted numbers, and a version already pinned (with
--no-write it continues; if the release now serves a different hash for a version already
pinned, it is refused either way).

ROOTS
=====
The descriptor of a service is <root>/<service>/image_bump.py. --root names the directory that
holds the service directories. Without it: `check` reads the source tree of the private layer,
named by IMAGE_BUMP_SOURCE_ROOT (default ~/.local/share/chezmoi-overlay/dot_dotlocal); the
other verbs read the deployed tree, named by IMAGE_BUMP_ROOT (default ~/.dotlocal).

DESCRIPTOR
==========
A Python module holding ASSIGNMENTS ONLY (the system Python on some machines is 3.9, so no
TOML reader is assumed), loaded by path. Every field below is required unless marked optional.

  image         str   image name without tag, e.g. "registry.example/acme/widget"
  release_line  str   "patch-of-pinned-major" or "github-latest-stable"
  release_api   dict  {"host": "codeberg" | "github", "repo": "owner/name"}
  git_tag       str   the version as a git tag; must contain "{version}", e.g. "v{version}"
  platform      str   the one platform whose manifest digest is pinned, "os/arch"
  pin_file      str   path of the pin file; relative paths are taken from the descriptor's
                      directory, and "~" is expanded
  scan_dir      str   directory the scan records are written to (same path rules)
  gate          dict  {"kind": "fixable-count-floor" | "no-new-findings"}
  migrations    dict  {"kind": "added-files", "path": "<repo path>"} or {"kind": "always"}
  volume        str   the data volume a migration would touch
  container     str   the container's name, whose running image `deploy` reads
  service       str   the systemd user unit inside the VM that runs it, e.g. "widget.service"
  vm_unit       str   the unit's path inside the VM
  install       dict  {"kind": "vm-copy", "applied_unit": "<path>"},
                      {"kind": "script", "path": "<path>"} or
                      {"kind": "manual", "steps": ["<one line a person runs or checks>", ...]}
  checks        list  of {"name": str, "command": [str, ...], "pass": str}, where "pass"
                      says what a pass is (an exit status, or text to find in the output)
  checklist     list  of str: lines printed for the person or agent to work through
  tag_suffix    str   optional; a suffix of the tag that is not part of the version, such as
                      "-rootless"

A manual install is for an image put in place by hand on a host this tool cannot drive (a host
whose container runtime is root-only, say). `check`, `latest` and `validate` work as for any container
descriptor, and `deploy` refuses with status 4, printing the steps. `deploy` is the only verb that
reads `volume`, `vm_unit`, `container`, `service` and `checks`, so a manual descriptor must not
have them (`validate` refuses one that does); `migrations` stays required, since `check` reads it
to write the scan record.

The version of the pinned image is its tag with `tag_suffix` and a leading "v" removed; its git
tag is `git_tag` with {version} replaced. `validate` also reads the pin file, which must parse
and must name the descriptor's image.

EXIT STATUSES
=============
  0  pass, or up to date          5  rolled back and up again; revert the pin
  2  usage or environment error   6  failed and not recovered (a migrating version, or a
                                    rollback that did not bring it back); stopped for a person
  3  exception the gate allows   7  a newer release exists (`latest`)
     only with the operator's
     approval
  4  refused (validate: a malformed descriptor; deploy: an artifact descriptor, or
                    a container installed by hand, whose steps it prints)
Status 1 is never used on purpose: an uncaught Python exception exits 1, so main() turns one
into a one-line message and status 2.

External commands are run without a shell: skopeo, trivy and podman for containers; for
artifacts cosign (cosign-bundle; it needs the sigstore trust root, which it fetches itself, so a
sandbox must let it write its TUF cache, named by TUF_ROOT), gpg and gpgv (apt-release).

Targets Python 3.9, standard library only.
"""

import argparse
import datetime
import hashlib
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import re
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time
import urllib.error
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request

OK, USAGE, APPROVAL, REFUSED, ROLLED_BACK, STOPPED, NEWER = 0, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7

RELEASE_LINES = ("patch-of-pinned-major", "github-latest-stable")
RELEASE_HOSTS = ("codeberg", "github")
GATE_KINDS = ("fixable-count-floor", "no-new-findings")
MIGRATION_KINDS = ("added-files", "always")
INSTALL_KINDS = ("vm-copy", "script", "manual")
# Fields only `deploy` reads; a container descriptor whose install kind is "manual" has none.
DEPLOY_ONLY = ("volume", "vm_unit", "container", "service", "checks")

# (field, what it is) for the plain string fields, validated by one loop.
STR_FIELDS = ("image", "git_tag", "platform", "pin_file", "scan_dir", "volume", "vm_unit",
              "container", "service")
REQUIRED = ("image", "release_line", "release_api", "git_tag", "platform", "pin_file",
            "scan_dir", "gate", "migrations", "volume", "vm_unit", "container", "service", "install",
            "checks", "checklist")

RELEASE_APIS = {
    "codeberg": ("IMAGE_BUMP_CODEBERG_API", "https://codeberg.org/api/v1"),
    "github": ("IMAGE_BUMP_GITHUB_API", "https://api.github.com"),
}
SEVERITIES = "HIGH,CRITICAL"

PLATFORM_RE = re.compile(r"^[a-z0-9_]+/[a-z0-9_]+$")
PIN_RE = re.compile(
    r"^(?P<image>[^\s@]+):(?P<tag>[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,127})"
    r"@sha256:(?P<digest>[0-9a-f]{64})$")


class EnvError(Exception):
    """The environment, not the request, is at fault: a command missing or failing, bad JSON."""


class DescriptorError(Exception):
    """A descriptor or pin file cannot be used; carries one line per problem."""

    def __init__(self, problems):
        Exception.__init__(self, "; ".join(problems))
        self.problems = list(problems)


def load_descriptor(root, service):
    """Load <root>/<service>/image_bump.py by path; return (module, directory)."""
    directory = os.path.join(root, service)
    path = os.path.join(directory, "image_bump.py")
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        raise DescriptorError(["no descriptor at %s" % path])
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("image_bump_descriptor_" + service, path)
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    spec.loader.exec_module(module)
    return module, directory


def resolve_path(value, directory):
    value = os.path.expanduser(value)
    return value if os.path.isabs(value) else os.path.join(directory, value)


def parse_pin(text):
    """Parse '<image>:<tag>@sha256:<64 hex>' into (image, tag, digest).

    Blank lines and '#' comment lines are ignored; exactly one pin line must remain.
    Raises ValueError with a one-line reason for anything else.
    """
    lines = [ln.strip() for ln in text.splitlines()]
    lines = [ln for ln in lines if ln and not ln.startswith("#")]
    if len(lines) != 1:
        raise ValueError("expected exactly one pin line, found %d" % len(lines))
    m = PIN_RE.match(lines[0])
    if not m:
        raise ValueError("not of the form <image>:<tag>@sha256:<64 hex>: %r" % lines[0])
    return m.group("image"), m.group("tag"), "sha256:" + m.group("digest")


def read_pin(path):
    """Read and parse a pin file; return (image, tag, digest)."""
    try:
        with open(path) as fh:
            text = fh.read()
    except OSError as exc:
        raise ValueError("cannot read pin file %s: %s" % (path, exc.strerror))
    return parse_pin(text)


def version_from_tag(tag, suffix=""):
    """The version a tag stands for: `suffix` and a leading 'v' removed."""
    if suffix and tag.endswith(suffix):
        tag = tag[:-len(suffix)]
    if tag.startswith("v"):
        tag = tag[1:]
    return tag


def git_tag_for(git_tag, version):
    """The git tag of a version, from the descriptor's template (e.g. 'v{version}')."""
    return git_tag.replace("{version}", version)


def _is_str(v):
    return isinstance(v, str) and v != ""


def _kind_problems(name, value, kinds, extra):
    """Problems of a {'kind': ...} dict; `extra` maps kind -> required string key or None."""
    if not isinstance(value, dict):
        return ["%s: expected a dict with a 'kind', got %s" % (name, type(value).__name__)]
    kind = value.get("kind")
    if kind not in kinds:
        return ["%s: unknown kind %r (known: %s)" % (name, kind, ", ".join(kinds))]
    key = extra.get(kind)
    if key and not _is_str(value.get(key)):
        return ["%s: kind %s needs a non-empty string %r" % (name, kind, key)]
    return []


def _release_problems(module):
    """Problems of the fields that say where releases are found: release_line, release_api and
    git_tag. Shared by container and artifact descriptors."""
    problems = []
    get = lambda n: getattr(module, n, None)
    if hasattr(module, "release_line") and get("release_line") not in RELEASE_LINES:
        problems.append("release_line: unknown strategy %r (known: %s)"
                        % (get("release_line"), ", ".join(RELEASE_LINES)))

    if hasattr(module, "release_api"):
        api = get("release_api")
        if not isinstance(api, dict):
            problems.append("release_api: expected a dict, got %s" % type(api).__name__)
        elif api.get("host") not in RELEASE_HOSTS:
            problems.append("release_api: unknown host %r (known: %s)"
                            % (api.get("host"), ", ".join(RELEASE_HOSTS)))
        elif not _is_str(api.get("repo")) or "/" not in api.get("repo"):
            problems.append("release_api: 'repo' must be a string like owner/name")

    if _is_str(get("git_tag")) and "{version}" not in get("git_tag"):
        problems.append("git_tag: must contain {version}, got %r" % get("git_tag"))
    return problems


def descriptor_problems(module, directory):
    """Every problem of a loaded descriptor, one string each; empty means valid."""
    if is_artifact(module):
        return artifact_problems(module, directory)
    problems = []
    get = lambda n: getattr(module, n, None)
    for name in ARTIFACT_FIELDS[1:]:
        if hasattr(module, name):
            problems.append("%s: only valid with an 'artifact'" % name)
    install = get("install")
    manual = isinstance(install, dict) and install.get("kind") == "manual"
    for name in REQUIRED:
        if not hasattr(module, name) and not (manual and name in DEPLOY_ONLY):
            problems.append("%s: missing" % name)
    if manual:
        for name in DEPLOY_ONLY:
            if hasattr(module, name):
                problems.append("%s: not valid with an install kind of manual" % name)
    for name in STR_FIELDS:
        if hasattr(module, name) and not manual and not _is_str(get(name)):
            problems.append("%s: expected a non-empty str, got %r" % (name, get(name)))

    problems += _release_problems(module)

    if _is_str(get("platform")) and not PLATFORM_RE.match(get("platform")):
        problems.append("platform: %r is not of the form os/arch" % get("platform"))

    if hasattr(module, "gate"):
        problems += _kind_problems("gate", get("gate"), GATE_KINDS, {})
    if hasattr(module, "migrations"):
        problems += _kind_problems("migrations", get("migrations"), MIGRATION_KINDS,
                                   {"added-files": "path"})
    if hasattr(module, "install"):
        problems += _kind_problems("install", get("install"), INSTALL_KINDS,
                                   {"vm-copy": "applied_unit", "script": "path"})
        if manual:
            steps = install.get("steps")
            if not (isinstance(steps, list) and steps and all(_is_str(x) for x in steps)):
                problems.append("install: kind manual needs a non-empty list of non-empty "
                                "strings in 'steps'")

    if hasattr(module, "checks") and not manual:
        checks = get("checks")
        if not isinstance(checks, list) or not checks:
            problems.append("checks: expected a non-empty list")
        else:
            for i, c in enumerate(checks):
                if not (isinstance(c, dict) and _is_str(c.get("name"))
                        and _is_str(c.get("pass")) and isinstance(c.get("command"), list)
                        and c["command"] and all(_is_str(a) for a in c["command"])):
                    problems.append("checks[%d]: expected {'name': str, 'command': "
                                    "[str, ...], 'pass': str}" % i)
    if hasattr(module, "checklist"):
        cl = get("checklist")
        if not isinstance(cl, list) or not all(_is_str(x) for x in cl):
            problems.append("checklist: expected a list of non-empty str")
    if hasattr(module, "tag_suffix") and not isinstance(get("tag_suffix"), str):
        problems.append("tag_suffix: expected a str, got %r" % get("tag_suffix"))

    if not problems:
        try:
            image, _tag, _digest = read_pin(resolve_path(get("pin_file"), directory))
        except ValueError as exc:
            problems.append("pin_file: %s" % exc)
        else:
            if image != get("image"):
                problems.append("pin_file: pins image %r, descriptor says %r"
                                % (image, get("image")))
    return problems


def default_root(verb):
    if verb == "check":
        return os.environ.get("IMAGE_BUMP_SOURCE_ROOT") or os.path.expanduser(
            "~/.local/share/chezmoi-overlay/dot_dotlocal")
    return os.environ.get("IMAGE_BUMP_ROOT") or os.path.expanduser("~/.dotlocal")


def cmd_validate(args):
    try:
        module, directory = load_descriptor(args.root, args.service)
    except DescriptorError as exc:
        for p in exc.problems:
            sys.stderr.write("image-bump: %s: %s\n" % (args.service, p))
        return REFUSED
    problems = descriptor_problems(module, directory)
    if problems:
        for p in problems:
            sys.stderr.write("image-bump: %s: %s\n" % (args.service, p))
        return REFUSED
    print("ok %s" % args.service)
    return OK


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- check

def run_cmd(argv):
    """Run an external command; return its stdout as BYTES (a manifest digest is the hash of
    the exact bytes, so no newline translation). Any failure is an EnvError."""
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(argv, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE)
    except OSError as exc:
        raise EnvError("cannot run %s: %s" % (argv[0], exc.strerror))
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        raise EnvError("%s failed (status %d): %s"
                       % (" ".join(argv[:3]), proc.returncode, proc.stderr.decode('utf-8', 'replace').strip()[:300]))
    return proc.stdout


def parse_json(text, what):
    try:
        return json.loads(text)
    except ValueError as exc:
        raise EnvError("%s: unreadable JSON (%s)" % (what, exc))


def resolve_digest(image, tag, platform):
    """The manifest digest of `platform` for image:tag; raises DescriptorError-like Refusal."""
    want_os, want_arch = platform.split("/", 1)
    raw = run_cmd(["skopeo", "inspect", "--raw", "docker://%s:%s" % (image, tag)])
    doc = parse_json(raw, "skopeo inspect --raw")
    if not isinstance(doc, dict):
        raise EnvError("skopeo inspect --raw: not a JSON object")
    if "manifests" in doc:
        for entry in doc["manifests"]:
            plat = entry.get("platform") or {}
            if plat.get("os") == want_os and plat.get("architecture") == want_arch:
                return entry["digest"]
        raise Refusal("%s:%s is an index with no %s entry" % (image, tag, platform))
    return "sha256:" + hashlib.sha256(raw).hexdigest()


def verify_platform(image, digest, platform):
    """Refuse when the image configuration behind `digest` is not for `platform`."""
    want_os, want_arch = platform.split("/", 1)
    conf = parse_json(run_cmd(["skopeo", "inspect", "--config",
                               "docker://%s@%s" % (image, digest)]), "skopeo inspect --config")
    got = "%s/%s" % (conf.get("os"), conf.get("architecture"))
    if got != platform:
        raise Refusal("%s@%s is built for %s, the descriptor says %s"
                      % (image, digest, got, platform))


class Refusal(Exception):
    """The request is refused (status 4); the message says why."""


def trivy_database():
    """Fetch the vulnerability database once; return (trivy version, database timestamp)."""
    run_cmd(["trivy", "image", "--download-db-only"])
    info = parse_json(run_cmd(["trivy", "version", "--format", "json"]), "trivy version")
    db = (info.get("VulnerabilityDB") or {}).get("UpdatedAt")
    if not db:
        raise EnvError("trivy version: no VulnerabilityDB.UpdatedAt")
    return info.get("Version", "unknown"), db


def scan(image, digest, platform):
    """Scan image@digest; return findings as dicts, de-duplicated, sorted."""
    out = run_cmd(["trivy", "image", "--skip-db-update", "--scanners", "vuln",
                   "--image-src", "remote", "--platform", platform,
                   "--severity", SEVERITIES, "--format", "json", "%s@%s" % (image, digest)])
    return findings_of(parse_json(out, "trivy image"))


def findings_of(doc):
    """The HIGH/CRITICAL findings of a trivy JSON report, de-duplicated and sorted."""
    seen, found = set(), []
    for result in doc.get("Results") or []:
        for v in result.get("Vulnerabilities") or []:
            f = {"id": v.get("VulnerabilityID", ""), "pkg": v.get("PkgName", ""),
                 "installed": v.get("InstalledVersion", ""), "fixed": v.get("FixedVersion", ""),
                 "severity": v.get("Severity", "")}
            key = (f["id"], f["pkg"], f["installed"])
            if key not in seen:
                seen.add(key)
                found.append(f)
    return sorted(found, key=lambda f: (f["id"], f["pkg"], f["installed"]))


def fixable(findings):
    return [f for f in findings if f["fixed"]]


def apply_gate(kind, new, cur, first=False):
    """Return (verdict, message): verdict is 'pass', 'exception' or 'refused'. `first` is a
    first pin: no current pin exists, so there is nothing to compare against."""
    if kind == "fixable-count-floor":
        n, c = len(fixable(new)), len(fixable(cur))
        if n == 0:
            return "pass", "no fixable finding (current pin: %d)" % c
        if first:
            return "exception", ("%d fixable findings in a first pin, which has no current pin "
                                 "to compare" % n)
        if n < c:
            return "exception", ("%d fixable findings, fewer than the current pin's %d" % (n, c))
        return "refused", ("%d fixable findings, not fewer than the current pin's %d" % (n, c))
    if kind == "no-new-findings":
        old = set((f["id"], f["pkg"]) for f in cur)
        added = sorted(set((f["id"], f["pkg"]) for f in new) - old)
        if not added:
            return "pass", "no finding the current pin lacks"
        return "refused", "new findings: " + ", ".join("%s (%s)" % a for a in added)
    raise EnvError("unknown gate kind %r" % kind)


def fetch_listing(base, repo, path, ref):
    """The names of the files in directory `path` of `repo` at git tag `ref`, from the release
    host's contents API: GET <base>/repos/<repo>/contents/<path>?ref=<ref>, a JSON list of
    objects with `name` and `type`. Anything else (a transport failure, a 404, a response that
    is not a list) is an EnvError: an unreadable listing is never read as "no migration"."""
    url = "%s/repos/%s/contents/%s?ref=%s" % (
        base, repo, urllib.parse.quote(path.strip("/")), urllib.parse.quote(ref, safe=""))
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(url, timeout=30) as resp:
            body = resp.read().decode("utf-8")
    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        raise EnvError("listing %s: %s" % (url, exc))
    doc = parse_json(body, "listing %s" % url)
    if not isinstance(doc, list):
        raise EnvError("listing %s: not a list of files" % url)
    return set(e.get("name") for e in doc
               if isinstance(e, dict) and e.get("type") == "file" and e.get("name"))


def detect_migration(desc, old_version, new_version):
    kind = desc.migrations["kind"]
    if kind == "always":
        return True
    env, default = RELEASE_APIS[desc.release_api["host"]]
    base = (os.environ.get(env) or default).rstrip("/")
    repo, path = desc.release_api["repo"], desc.migrations["path"]
    old = fetch_listing(base, repo, path, git_tag_for(desc.git_tag, old_version))
    new = fetch_listing(base, repo, path, git_tag_for(desc.git_tag, new_version))
    return bool(new - old)


def table(findings):
    rows = [("VULNERABILITY", "PACKAGE", "INSTALLED", "FIXED", "SEVERITY")]
    rows += [(f["id"], f["pkg"], f["installed"], f["fixed"] or "-", f["severity"])
             for f in findings]
    widths = [max(len(r[i]) for r in rows) for i in range(5)]
    return "\n".join("  ".join(c.ljust(w) for c, w in zip(r, widths)).rstrip() for r in rows)


def render_record(name, header, new, cur):
    lines = ["%s image CVE scan record" % name]
    lines += ["%s: %s" % kv for kv in header]
    lines.append("")
    for title, digest, fs in (("New image", header[1][1], new), ("Current pin", header[3][1], cur)):
        lines.append("%s findings (%s): %d total, %d fixable" % (title, digest, len(fs),
                                                                  len(fixable(fs))))
        lines.append(table(fs))
        lines.append("")
    return "\n".join(lines)


def read_record(path):
    """The header of a scan record as a dict: the `key: value` lines before the first blank."""
    with open(path) as fh:
        lines = fh.read().splitlines()
    header = {}
    for line in lines[1:]:
        if not line.strip():
            break
        key, sep, value = line.partition(": ")
        if sep:
            header[key] = value
    return header


def check_service(args, desc, directory):
    pin_path = resolve_path(desc.pin_file, directory)
    image, cur_tag, cur_digest = read_pin(pin_path)
    suffix = getattr(desc, "tag_suffix", "")
    old_version = version_from_tag(cur_tag, suffix)
    if not old_version or old_version not in cur_tag:
        raise Refusal("cannot find version %r in the current tag %r" % (old_version, cur_tag))
    new_tag = cur_tag.replace(old_version, args.version, 1)
    platform = desc.platform

    digest = resolve_digest(image, new_tag, platform)
    verify_platform(image, digest, platform)
    print("resolved %s:%s -> %s" % (image, new_tag, digest))
    if digest == cur_digest:
        if not args.no_write:
            raise Refusal("%s:%s is already pinned (%s)" % (image, new_tag, digest))
        print("note: already pinned; continuing because of --no-write")

    scanner, database = trivy_database()
    new = scan(image, digest, platform)
    cur = scan(image, cur_digest, platform)
    verdict, why = apply_gate(desc.gate["kind"], new, cur)
    print("gate %s: %s: %s" % (desc.gate["kind"], verdict, why))
    if verdict == "refused":
        raise Refusal("gate %s: %s" % (desc.gate["kind"], why))

    migrates = detect_migration(desc, old_version, args.version)
    print("migrates: %s" % ("yes" if migrates else "no"))

    name = image.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
    now = datetime.datetime.now().astimezone()
    header = [("image", "%s:%s" % (image, new_tag)), ("digest", digest), ("platform", platform),
              ("replaces", cur_digest), ("scanner", scanner), ("database", database),
              ("scanned", "%s %s" % (now.isoformat(timespec="seconds"), socket.gethostname())),
              ("gate", desc.gate["kind"]), ("verdict", verdict),
              ("migrates", "yes" if migrates else "no")]
    if args.no_write:
        print("not written (--no-write)")
    else:
        scan_dir = resolve_path(desc.scan_dir, directory)
        record = os.path.join(scan_dir, "%s-%s-%s.txt" % (name, args.version,
                                                          now.strftime("%Y-%m-%d")))
        if os.path.exists(record):
            raise Refusal("scan record %s exists; records are never overwritten" % record)
        os.makedirs(scan_dir, exist_ok=True)
        with open(record, "w") as fh:
            fh.write(render_record(name, header, new, cur))
        tmp = pin_path + ".tmp"
        with open(tmp, "w") as fh:
            fh.write("%s:%s@%s\n" % (image, new_tag, digest))
        os.replace(tmp, pin_path)
        print("wrote %s and %s" % (record, pin_path))

    for line in desc.checklist:
        print("checklist: %s" % line)
    if verdict == "exception":
        print("APPROVAL: %s:%s carries %d fixable HIGH/CRITICAL findings (%d in all); the "
              "current pin carries %d (%d in all). This needs the operator's explicit approval."
              % (image, new_tag, len(fixable(new)), len(new), len(fixable(cur)), len(cur)))
        return APPROVAL
    return OK


def get_json(url):
    """GET `url` and parse the body as JSON. A transport failure, an HTTP error status or a body
    that is not JSON is an EnvError: failing to read is never an answer."""
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(url, timeout=30) as resp:
            body = resp.read().decode("utf-8")
    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        raise EnvError("reading %s: %s" % (url, exc))
    return parse_json(body, url)


VERSION_RE = re.compile(r"^[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)*$")


def numeric_version(text):
    """(1, 2, 3) for "1.2.3"; None for anything that is not purely numeric and dotted."""
    if not isinstance(text, str) or not VERSION_RE.match(text):
        return None
    return tuple(int(n) for n in text.split("."))


def version_of_git_tag(template, tag):
    """The numeric version tuple a git tag stands for under the descriptor's `git_tag` template
    (e.g. "v{version}"), or None when the tag does not fit the template or is not numeric."""
    head, _, tail = template.partition("{version}")
    if not isinstance(tag, str) or len(tag) < len(head) + len(tail):
        return None
    if not tag.startswith(head) or (tail and not tag.endswith(tail)):
        return None
    middle = tag[len(head):len(tag) - len(tail)]
    return numeric_version(middle)


def dotted(version):
    return ".".join(str(n) for n in version)


def latest_service(args, desc, directory):
    if is_artifact(desc):
        text = args.pin if args.pin is not None else pinned_version(desc)
        if text is None:
            raise EnvError("the pins files hold no version yet (a placeholder), so there is "
                           "nothing to compare; pass --pin VERSION")
    else:
        try:
            _image, cur_tag, _digest = read_pin(resolve_path(desc.pin_file, directory))
        except ValueError as exc:
            raise EnvError(str(exc))
        text = args.pin if args.pin is not None else version_from_tag(
            cur_tag, getattr(desc, "tag_suffix", ""))
    current = numeric_version(text)
    if current is None:
        raise EnvError("the %s version %r is not a numeric dotted version"
                       % ("--pin" if args.pin is not None else "pinned", text))
    host = desc.release_api["host"]
    env, default = RELEASE_APIS[host]
    base = (os.environ.get(env) or default).rstrip("/")
    repo = desc.release_api["repo"]
    template = desc.git_tag
    newest = None
    if desc.release_line == "github-latest-stable":
        url = "%s/repos/%s/releases/latest" % (base, repo)
        doc = get_json(url)
        if not isinstance(doc, dict):
            raise EnvError("%s: not a release object" % url)
        found = version_of_git_tag(template, doc.get("tag_name"))
        if found is None:
            raise EnvError("%s: tag %r is not a numeric version under %r"
                           % (url, doc.get("tag_name"), template))
        if found > current:
            newest = found
    else:
        query = "limit=50" if host == "codeberg" else "per_page=50"
        url = "%s/repos/%s/releases?%s" % (base, repo, query)
        doc = get_json(url)
        if not isinstance(doc, list):
            raise EnvError("%s: not a list of releases" % url)
        for rel in doc:
            if not isinstance(rel, dict):
                raise EnvError("%s: a release is not an object" % url)
            if rel.get("draft") or rel.get("prerelease"):
                continue
            found = version_of_git_tag(template, rel.get("tag_name"))
            if found is None or found[0] != current[0] or found <= current:
                continue
            if newest is None or found > newest:
                newest = found
    shown = dotted(current)
    if newest is None:
        print("up to date %s %s" % (args.service, shown))
        return OK
    print("newer %s %s -> %s (%s)" % (args.service, shown, dotted(newest),
                                      git_tag_for(template, dotted(newest))))
    return NEWER


def cmd_latest(args):
    return with_descriptor(args, latest_service)


def cmd_check(args):
    return with_descriptor(args, check_dispatch)


def check_dispatch(args, desc, directory):
    work = check_artifact if is_artifact(desc) else check_service
    return work(args, desc, directory)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- artifacts
# An image build's pinned inputs file holds, besides container images, `file` and `deb` lines:
# a binary or a Debian package fetched from a URL and checked against a sha256. An artifact
# descriptor moves those lines. Its hash is trusted only after a signature has been verified, and
# both architectures are resolved, verified, downloaded and scanned before either file is touched.

ARTIFACT_KINDS = ("release-file", "release-deb")
VERIFY_KINDS = ("cosign-bundle", "apt-release")
# Fields only an artifact descriptor has; the first names the kind of descriptor.
ARTIFACT_FIELDS = ("artifact", "arches", "pins_files", "verify")
ARTIFACT_REQUIRED = ARTIFACT_FIELDS + ("release_line", "release_api", "git_tag", "scan_dir",
                                       "gate", "checklist")
# Fields of a container descriptor; an artifact descriptor has none of them.
CONTAINER_FIELDS = ("image", "platform", "pin_file", "migrations", "volume", "container",
                    "service", "vm_unit", "install", "checks", "tag_suffix")
VERSION_CORE = r"[0-9]+(?:\.[0-9]+)*"
FINGERPRINT_RE = re.compile(r"^[0-9A-F]{40}$")
SHA256_RE = re.compile(r"^[0-9a-f]{64}$")
DEB_NAME_RE = re.compile(r"^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9+.-]+$")
PINS_LINE_RE = re.compile(r"^(?P<kind>file|deb)[ \t]+(?P<name>\S+)[ \t]+(?P<url>\S+)[ \t]+"
                          r"(?P<hash>\S+)(?P<tail>[ \t]*(?:#.*)?)$")
PLACEHOLDER = "PLACEHOLDER"
DOWNLOAD_BASE = ("IMAGE_BUMP_GITHUB_DOWNLOAD", "https://github.com")


def is_artifact(module):
    return hasattr(module, "artifact")


def url_ok(url):
    """A pinned URL is https; plain http is allowed only to the loopback, which the tests use."""
    return isinstance(url, str) and (url.startswith("https://") or url.startswith("http://127.0.0.1"))


def _asset_problems(name, value):
    if not _is_str(value) or "{version}" not in value or "{arch}" not in value:
        return ["%s: must be a string containing both {version} and {arch}, got %r" % (name, value)]
    return []


def _str_key_problems(name, value, keys):
    return ["%s: needs a non-empty string %r" % (name, k) for k in keys if not _is_str(value.get(k))]


def artifact_problems(module, directory):
    """Every problem of an artifact descriptor, one string each; empty means valid."""
    problems = []
    get = lambda n: getattr(module, n, None)
    for name in ARTIFACT_REQUIRED:
        if not hasattr(module, name):
            problems.append("%s: missing" % name)
    for name in CONTAINER_FIELDS:
        if hasattr(module, name):
            problems.append("%s: not used by an artifact descriptor" % name)
    problems += _release_problems(module)
    if hasattr(module, "scan_dir") and not _is_str(get("scan_dir")):
        problems.append("scan_dir: expected a non-empty str, got %r" % get("scan_dir"))
    if hasattr(module, "gate"):
        problems += _kind_problems("gate", get("gate"), GATE_KINDS, {})
    if hasattr(module, "checklist"):
        cl = get("checklist")
        if not isinstance(cl, list) or not all(_is_str(x) for x in cl):
            problems.append("checklist: expected a list of non-empty str")

    art, kind = get("artifact"), None
    if hasattr(module, "artifact"):
        if not isinstance(art, dict) or art.get("kind") not in ARTIFACT_KINDS:
            problems.append("artifact: expected a dict whose kind is one of %s"
                            % ", ".join(ARTIFACT_KINDS))
        else:
            kind = art["kind"]
            if kind == "release-file":
                if not _is_str(art.get("path")) or not art["path"].startswith("/"):
                    problems.append("artifact: kind release-file needs an absolute 'path'")
                if "sbom" in art:
                    problems += _asset_problems("artifact.sbom", art["sbom"])
            else:
                if not _is_str(art.get("package")) or not DEB_NAME_RE.match(art["package"]):
                    problems.append("artifact: kind release-deb needs a Debian package name in "
                                    "'package'")
            problems += _asset_problems("artifact.asset", art.get("asset"))

    arches, pins = get("arches"), get("pins_files")
    for name, value in (("arches", arches), ("pins_files", pins)):
        if hasattr(module, name) and (not isinstance(value, dict) or not value
                                      or not all(_is_str(k) and _is_str(v)
                                                 for k, v in value.items())):
            problems.append("%s: expected a non-empty dict of non-empty strings" % name)
    if (isinstance(arches, dict) and isinstance(pins, dict)
            and set(arches) != set(pins)):
        problems.append("pins_files: must have the same keys as arches (%s)"
                        % ", ".join(sorted(arches)))

    verify = get("verify")
    vkind = None
    if hasattr(module, "verify"):
        if not isinstance(verify, dict) or verify.get("kind") not in VERIFY_KINDS:
            problems.append("verify: expected a dict whose kind is one of %s"
                            % ", ".join(VERIFY_KINDS))
        else:
            vkind = verify["kind"]
            if vkind == "cosign-bundle":
                problems += _str_key_problems("verify", verify, (
                    "checksums", "bundle", "certificate_identity", "certificate_oidc_issuer"))
            else:
                problems += _str_key_problems("verify", verify, (
                    "repo", "suite", "component", "fingerprint", "key_file"))
                if _is_str(verify.get("repo")) and not url_ok(verify["repo"]):
                    problems.append("verify: 'repo' must be an https URL")
                fpr = verify.get("fingerprint")
                if _is_str(fpr) and not FINGERPRINT_RE.match(fpr.replace(" ", "").upper()):
                    problems.append("verify: 'fingerprint' must be 40 hex digits")
                if _is_str(verify.get("key_file")) and not os.path.isfile(
                        resolve_path(verify["key_file"], directory)):
                    problems.append("verify: key_file %s does not exist" % verify["key_file"])
    if kind and vkind and (kind, vkind) not in (("release-file", "cosign-bundle"),
                                                ("release-deb", "apt-release")):
        problems.append("verify: kind %s cannot verify an artifact of kind %s (supported: "
                        "release-file with cosign-bundle, release-deb with apt-release)"
                        % (vkind, kind))

    if not problems:
        for arch_key, path in sorted(pins.items()):
            try:
                read_pins(resolve_path(path, os.getcwd()), art)
            except (Refusal, EnvError) as exc:
                problems.append("pins_files.%s: %s" % (arch_key, exc))
    return problems


def pins_key(art):
    """(line kind, name) of the pins line an artifact owns."""
    if art["kind"] == "release-file":
        return "file", art["path"]
    return "deb", art["package"]


def read_pins(path, art):
    """(lines, index, match) of the one pins line the artifact owns in the file at `path`.
    Anything but exactly one such line is a Refusal."""
    try:
        with open(path) as fh:
            lines = fh.read().splitlines(True)
    except OSError as exc:
        raise EnvError("cannot read pins file %s: %s" % (path, exc.strerror))
    want = pins_key(art)
    found = []
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        m = PINS_LINE_RE.match(line.rstrip("\r\n"))
        if m and (m.group("kind"), m.group("name")) == want:
            found.append((i, m))
    if len(found) != 1:
        raise Refusal("%s has %d `%s %s` lines, expected exactly one" % (path, len(found), *want))
    return lines, found[0][0], found[0][1]


def asset_name(template, version, arch):
    return template.replace("{version}", version).replace("{arch}", arch)


def version_in_filename(template, arch, filename):
    """The version `filename` carries under an asset template, or None when it does not fit."""
    out, seen = [], False
    for part in re.split(r"(\{version\}|\{arch\})", template):
        if part == "{version}":
            out.append("(?P=v)" if seen else "(?P<v>%s)" % VERSION_CORE)
            seen = True
        elif part == "{arch}":
            out.append(re.escape(arch))
        else:
            out.append(re.escape(part))
    m = re.match("^%s$" % "".join(out), filename)
    return m.group("v") if m else None


def pinned_state(desc, arch_key):
    """What an architecture's pins line holds now: {'url', 'hash', 'version'}; each is None
    while the line says PLACEHOLDER (or, for the version, names something else)."""
    art = desc.artifact
    _lines, _i, m = read_pins(resolve_path(desc.pins_files[arch_key], os.getcwd()), art)
    url, digest = m.group("url"), m.group("hash")
    state = {"url": None, "hash": None, "version": None}
    if url != PLACEHOLDER:
        state["url"] = url
        state["version"] = version_in_filename(art["asset"], desc.arches[arch_key],
                                               url.rsplit("/", 1)[-1])
        if state["version"] is None:
            raise Refusal("the pinned URL %s does not fit the asset %s" % (url, art["asset"]))
    if digest.startswith("sha256:") and SHA256_RE.match(digest[7:]):
        state["hash"] = digest[7:]
    elif digest != "sha256:" + PLACEHOLDER:
        raise Refusal("the pinned hash %r is neither sha256:<64 hex> nor sha256:PLACEHOLDER"
                      % digest)
    return state


def pinned_version(desc):
    """The version the pins files name, read from the arm line's URL (the first architecture
    when none is called arm); None while it is a placeholder. The architectures must agree."""
    keys = sorted(desc.arches)
    versions = dict((k, pinned_state(desc, k)["version"]) for k in keys)
    real = set(v for v in versions.values() if v is not None)
    if len(real) > 1:
        raise Refusal("the pins files disagree on the version: %s"
                      % ", ".join("%s %s" % kv for kv in sorted(versions.items())))
    return versions["arm" if "arm" in versions else keys[0]] or next(iter(real), None)


class HttpMissing(EnvError):
    """A server answered 404."""


def download(url, dest):
    """Fetch `url` to the file `dest`; return the sha256 hex of the bytes. Only http(s)."""
    if not url.startswith(("http://", "https://")):
        raise EnvError("refusing to fetch %s: not an http(s) URL" % url)
    digest, size = hashlib.sha256(), 0
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(url, timeout=60) as resp, open(dest, "wb") as out:
            while True:
                chunk = resp.read(1 << 20)
                if not chunk:
                    break
                digest.update(chunk)
                size += len(chunk)
                out.write(chunk)
            announced = resp.headers.get("Content-Length")
        if announced is not None and announced.isdigit() and int(announced) != size:
            # http.client does not report a body that ends early; a cut-off transfer is the
            # environment's fault, not evidence about the release.
            raise EnvError("fetching %s: got %d bytes of the %s announced" % (url, size, announced))
    except urllib.error.HTTPError as exc:
        cls = HttpMissing if exc.code == 404 else EnvError
        raise cls("fetching %s: HTTP %d" % (url, exc.code))
    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        raise EnvError("fetching %s: %s" % (url, exc))
    return digest.hexdigest()


def run_status(argv):
    """Run a command with no shell; return (status, stdout bytes, stderr text). A command that
    cannot start is an EnvError; any exit status is the caller's to judge."""
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(argv, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE)
    except OSError as exc:
        raise EnvError("cannot run %s: %s" % (argv[0], exc.strerror))
    return proc.returncode, proc.stdout, proc.stderr.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()[:400]


def release_base(desc, version):
    env, default = DOWNLOAD_BASE
    base = (os.environ.get(env) or default).rstrip("/")
    return "%s/%s/releases/download/%s/" % (base, desc.release_api["repo"],
                                           git_tag_for(desc.git_tag, version))


def parse_checksums(text):
    """{file name: sha256 hex} from a `<hex>  <name>` checksums file; a name listed twice with
    different hashes is dropped from the answer by being mapped to None."""
    out = {}
    for line in text.splitlines():
        parts = line.split(None, 1)
        if len(parts) != 2 or not SHA256_RE.match(parts[0]):
            continue
        name = parts[1].strip().lstrip("*")
        out[name] = None if name in out and out[name] != parts[0] else parts[0]
    return out


def resolve_cosign(desc, directory, version, work):
    """Verify the release's checksums file by its sigstore bundle, then take each architecture's
    hash from it and check the downloaded asset against that hash.
    Returns ({arch key: {'url', 'hash', 'file'}}, the `verify:` record line)."""
    v = desc.verify
    base = release_base(desc, version)
    names = {"checksums": asset_name(v["checksums"], version, ""),
             "bundle": asset_name(v["bundle"], version, "")}
    files = {}
    for what, name in sorted(names.items()):
        files[what] = os.path.join(work, what)
        download(base + urllib.parse.quote(name), files[what])
    identity = v["certificate_identity"].replace("{version}", version)
    issuer = v["certificate_oidc_issuer"]
    status, _out, err = run_status([
        "cosign", "verify-blob", "--bundle", files["bundle"],
        "--certificate-identity", identity, "--certificate-oidc-issuer", issuer,
        files["checksums"]])
    if status != 0:
        raise Refusal("cosign verify-blob refused %s (status %d): %s" % (names["checksums"], status, err))
    print("verified %s by %s (identity %s)" % (names["checksums"], names["bundle"], identity))
    with open(files["checksums"], encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
        sums = parse_checksums(fh.read())
    resolved = {}
    for arch_key in sorted(desc.arches):
        name = asset_name(desc.artifact["asset"], version, desc.arches[arch_key])
        want = sums.get(name)
        if want is None:
            raise Refusal("%s %s" % (names["checksums"], "lists %s twice with different hashes" % name
                                     if name in sums else "does not list %s" % name))
        dest = os.path.join(work, "asset-" + arch_key)
        url = base + urllib.parse.quote(name)
        got = download(url, dest)
        if got != want:
            raise Refusal("%s: the downloaded bytes hash to %s, the verified checksums say %s"
                          % (name, got, want))
        resolved[arch_key] = {"url": url, "hash": want, "file": dest}
    note = "cosign-bundle: %s signed by %s via %s" % (names["checksums"], identity, issuer)
    return resolved, note


def gpg_home(work):
    home = os.path.join(work, "gnupg")
    os.makedirs(home, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    return home


def pinned_keyring(key_file, fingerprint, work):
    """A keyring file holding only the key `key_file` carries, after proving that the file holds
    exactly one primary key and that its fingerprint is the pinned one. A key is trusted because
    its fingerprint is in the descriptor, never because it is in a file."""
    home = gpg_home(work)
    gpg = ["gpg", "--homedir", home, "--batch", "--no-autostart"]
    listing = run_cmd(gpg + ["--show-keys", "--with-colons", key_file]).decode("utf-8", "replace")
    primaries, expect_fpr = [], False
    for line in listing.splitlines():
        fields = line.split(":")
        if fields[0] == "pub":
            primaries.append(None)
            expect_fpr = True
        elif fields[0] == "fpr" and expect_fpr:
            primaries[-1] = fields[9].upper()
            expect_fpr = False
    want = fingerprint.replace(" ", "").upper()
    if primaries != [want]:
        raise Refusal("the key file %s holds keys %s, not exactly the pinned %s"
                      % (key_file, primaries or "none", want))
    run_cmd(gpg + ["--import", key_file])
    ring = os.path.join(work, "keyring.gpg")
    with open(ring, "wb") as fh:
        fh.write(run_cmd(gpg + ["--export"]))
    return ring, want


def verify_release(desc, directory, base, work):
    """Fetch and verify the suite's Release with the pinned key; return its text."""
    v = desc.verify
    ring, fpr = pinned_keyring(resolve_path(v["key_file"], directory), v["fingerprint"], work)
    sig = os.path.join(work, "Release.gpg")
    try:
        download(base + "Release.gpg", sig)
    except HttpMissing:
        # No detached signature: the clearsigned InRelease carries the text and the signature.
        inrelease = os.path.join(work, "InRelease")
        download(base + "InRelease", inrelease)
        text_file = os.path.join(work, "InRelease.text")
        argv = ["gpgv", "--keyring", ring, "--status-fd", "1", "--output", text_file, inrelease]
    else:
        release = os.path.join(work, "Release")
        download(base + "Release", release)
        text_file = release
        argv = ["gpgv", "--keyring", ring, "--status-fd", "1", sig, release]
    status, out, err = run_status(argv)
    signers = [ln.split()[-1].upper() for ln in out.decode("utf-8", "replace").splitlines()
               if ln.startswith("[GNUPG:] VALIDSIG ")]
    if status != 0 or fpr not in signers:
        raise Refusal("the suite's Release is not signed by the pinned key %s (gpgv status %d): %s"
                      % (fpr, status, err))
    with open(text_file, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
        return fh.read()


def release_hashes(text):
    """{path: (sha256 hex, size)} from the SHA256 section of an apt Release."""
    out, inside = {}, False
    for line in text.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("SHA256:"):
            inside = True
        elif inside and line[:1] in (" ", "\t"):
            parts = line.split()
            if len(parts) == 3 and SHA256_RE.match(parts[0]) and parts[1].isdigit():
                out[parts[2]] = (parts[0], int(parts[1]))
        else:
            inside = False
    return out


def packages_stanzas(text):
    """The `Key: value` fields of each stanza of a Packages index (continuations ignored)."""
    for block in re.split(r"\n\s*\n", text):
        fields = {}
        for line in block.splitlines():
            if line[:1] not in (" ", "\t") and ": " in line:
                key, _, value = line.partition(": ")
                fields[key] = value.strip()
        if fields:
            yield fields


def resolve_apt(desc, directory, version, work):
    """Verify the suite's Release by the pinned key, follow its hash to each architecture's
    Packages index, take the package's SHA256 and Filename from there and check the .deb.
    Returns ({arch key: {'url', 'hash', 'file'}}, the `verify:` record line)."""
    v = desc.verify
    repo = v["repo"].rstrip("/")
    base = "%s/dists/%s/" % (repo, v["suite"])
    hashes = release_hashes(verify_release(desc, directory, base, work))
    print("verified the Release of %s %s by key %s" % (repo, v["suite"], v["fingerprint"]))
    resolved = {}
    for arch_key in sorted(desc.arches):
        arch = desc.arches[arch_key]
        wanted = asset_name(desc.artifact["asset"], version, arch)
        rel = None
        for suffix in ("", ".gz", ".xz"):
            cand = "%s/binary-%s/Packages%s" % (v["component"], arch, suffix)
            if cand in hashes:
                rel = cand
                break
        if rel is None:
            raise Refusal("the Release lists no Packages index for %s/binary-%s"
                          % (v["component"], arch))
        packed = os.path.join(work, "Packages-" + arch_key)
        got = download(base + rel, packed)
        with open(packed, "rb") as fh:
            raw = fh.read()
        if got != hashes[rel][0] or len(raw) != hashes[rel][1]:
            raise Refusal("%s does not match the hash the signed Release gives it" % rel)
        if rel.endswith(".gz"):
            import gzip
            raw = gzip.decompress(raw)
        elif rel.endswith(".xz"):
            import lzma
            raw = lzma.decompress(raw)
        entries = [f for f in packages_stanzas(raw.decode("utf-8", "replace"))
                   if f.get("Package") == desc.artifact["package"]
                   and f.get("Architecture") == arch
                   and os.path.basename(f.get("Filename", "")) == wanted]
        if len(entries) != 1:
            raise Refusal("%s lists %d entries for %s (%s)" % (rel, len(entries), wanted, arch))
        entry = entries[0]
        filename, want = entry["Filename"], entry.get("SHA256", "")
        if filename.startswith("/") or ".." in filename.split("/") or not SHA256_RE.match(want):
            raise Refusal("%s: the entry for %s has an unusable Filename or SHA256" % (rel, wanted))
        dest = os.path.join(work, "asset-" + arch_key)
        url = "%s/%s" % (repo, urllib.parse.quote(filename))
        got = download(url, dest)
        if got != want:
            raise Refusal("%s: the downloaded bytes hash to %s, the signed Packages index says %s"
                          % (wanted, got, want))
        resolved[arch_key] = {"url": url, "hash": want, "file": dest}
    note = ("apt-release: Release of %s %s signed by key %s; the Packages hash is from the "
            "Release, the .deb hash from Packages" % (repo, v["suite"], v["fingerprint"]))
    return resolved, note


def extract_deb(path, dest):
    """Unpack the files of a .deb (an ar archive whose data.tar.* member is a tar) under `dest`,
    regular files and directories only, refusing a member that would land outside it."""
    import io
    import tarfile
    with open(path, "rb") as fh:
        blob = fh.read()
    if blob[:8] != b"!<arch>\n":
        raise Refusal("%s is not a .deb (no ar header)" % os.path.basename(path))
    pos, data = 8, None
    while pos + 60 <= len(blob):
        name = blob[pos:pos + 16].decode("ascii", "replace").strip().rstrip("/")
        try:
            size = int(blob[pos + 48:pos + 58])
        except ValueError:
            raise Refusal("%s: a damaged ar header" % os.path.basename(path))
        body = blob[pos + 60:pos + 60 + size]
        if name.startswith("data.tar"):
            data = (name, body)
            break
        pos += 60 + size + (size % 2)
    if data is None:
        raise Refusal("%s has no data.tar member" % os.path.basename(path))
    if data[0].endswith(".zst"):
        raise EnvError("the data member is zstd-compressed, which Python 3.9 cannot read")
    with tarfile.open(fileobj=io.BytesIO(data[1]), mode="r:*") as tf:
        for member in tf:
            parts = [p for p in member.name.split("/") if p not in ("", ".")]
            if ".." in parts or member.name.startswith("/"):
                raise Refusal("the package holds a member that escapes its directory: %s"
                              % member.name)
            target = os.path.join(dest, *parts)
            if member.isdir():
                os.makedirs(target, exist_ok=True)
            elif member.isreg():
                os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(target), exist_ok=True)
                with open(target, "wb") as out:
                    out.write(tf.extractfile(member).read())
                os.chmod(target, 0o755 if member.mode & 0o111 else 0o644)


def unpack(desc, file, dest):
    """Lay an artifact out as a root filesystem under `dest` for trivy to read."""
    os.makedirs(dest, exist_ok=True)
    art = desc.artifact
    if art["kind"] == "release-file":
        target = os.path.join(dest, art["path"].lstrip("/"))
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(target), exist_ok=True)
        with open(file, "rb") as src, open(target, "wb") as out:
            out.write(src.read())
        os.chmod(target, 0o755)
    else:
        extract_deb(file, dest)


def scan_rootfs(directory):
    """Scan an unpacked artifact; return (findings, number of packages trivy detected)."""
    out = run_cmd(["trivy", "rootfs", "--skip-db-update", "--scanners", "vuln",
                   "--severity", SEVERITIES, "--format", "json", "--list-all-pkgs", directory])
    doc = parse_json(out, "trivy rootfs")
    found = findings_of(doc)
    packages = sum(len(r.get("Packages") or []) for r in doc.get("Results") or [])
    return found, max(packages, len(found))


def scan_artifacts(desc, files, work, label):
    """Scan every architecture's file; return (findings merged, {arch key: package count})."""
    merged, counts = {}, {}
    for arch_key in sorted(files):
        root = os.path.join(work, "root-%s-%s" % (label, arch_key))
        unpack(desc, files[arch_key], root)
        found, counts[arch_key] = scan_rootfs(root)
        for f in found:
            merged[(f["id"], f["pkg"], f["installed"])] = f
    return sorted(merged.values(), key=lambda f: (f["id"], f["pkg"], f["installed"])), counts


def render_artifact_record(name, header, new, cur, version, old):
    lines = ["%s %s CVE scan record" % (name, header[0][1].split()[0])]
    lines += ["%s: %s" % kv for kv in header]
    lines.append("")
    for title, fs in (("New artifact findings (%s)" % version, new),
                      ("Current pin findings (%s)" % (old or "none"), cur)):
        lines.append("%s: %d total, %d fixable" % (title, len(fs), len(fixable(fs))))
        lines.append(table(fs))
        lines.append("")
    return "\n".join(lines)


def check_artifact(args, desc, directory):
    art = desc.artifact
    if not VERSION_RE.match(args.version):
        raise Refusal("%r is not a dotted numeric version" % args.version)
    keys = sorted(desc.arches)
    old_version = pinned_version(desc)
    states = dict((k, pinned_state(desc, k)) for k in keys)
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix="image-bump-") as work:
        resolve = resolve_cosign if desc.verify["kind"] == "cosign-bundle" else resolve_apt
        resolved, note = resolve(desc, directory, args.version, work)
        for k in keys:
            if not url_ok(resolved[k]["url"]):
                raise Refusal("%s: refusing to pin the non-https URL %s" % (k, resolved[k]["url"]))
            print("resolved %s %s sha256:%s" % (k, resolved[k]["url"], resolved[k]["hash"]))
        if old_version == args.version:
            same = all(states[k]["hash"] == resolved[k]["hash"] for k in keys)
            if not same:
                raise Refusal("version %s is already pinned, but with a different hash than the "
                              "release serves now: a re-published release needs a person" % old_version)
            if not args.no_write:
                raise Refusal("version %s is already pinned" % old_version)
            print("note: already pinned; continuing because of --no-write")

        current = {}
        for k in keys:
            st = states[k]
            if st["url"] and st["hash"]:
                dest = os.path.join(work, "current-" + k)
                got = download(st["url"], dest)
                if got != st["hash"]:
                    raise Refusal("the current pin of %s no longer matches its own hash (%s)"
                                  % (k, st["url"]))
                current[k] = dest

        scanner, database = trivy_database()
        new, new_counts = scan_artifacts(desc, dict((k, resolved[k]["file"]) for k in keys),
                                         work, "new")
        cur, _counts = scan_artifacts(desc, current, work, "cur") if current else ([], {})
        verdict, why = apply_gate(desc.gate["kind"], new, cur, first=not current)
        uncovered = sorted(k for k in keys if new_counts[k] == 0)
        print("gate %s: %s: %s" % (desc.gate["kind"], verdict, why))
        if verdict == "refused":
            raise Refusal("gate %s: %s" % (desc.gate["kind"], why))
        if uncovered:
            print("NO COVERAGE: trivy detected no package in the %s artifact; its scan says "
                  "nothing about it" % ", ".join(uncovered))
            verdict = "exception"

        sboms = {}
        if art.get("sbom"):
            base = release_base(desc, args.version)
            for k in keys:
                name = asset_name(art["sbom"], args.version, desc.arches[k])
                sboms[k] = (name, download(base + urllib.parse.quote(name),
                                           os.path.join(work, "sbom-" + k)))

    name = os.path.basename(art["path"]) if art["kind"] == "release-file" else art["package"]
    now = datetime.datetime.now().astimezone()
    header = [("artifact", "%s %s" % pins_key(art)), ("version", args.version), ("verify", note)]
    for k in keys:
        header.append((k, "%s sha256:%s" % (resolved[k]["url"], resolved[k]["hash"])))
    for k in sorted(sboms):
        header.append(("sbom-" + k, "%s sha256:%s (not covered by the verified checksums)"
                       % sboms[k]))
    header += [("replaces", old_version or "none"), ("scanner", scanner), ("database", database),
               ("scanned", "%s %s" % (now.isoformat(timespec="seconds"), socket.gethostname())),
               ("gate", desc.gate["kind"]), ("verdict", verdict),
               ("packages", ", ".join("%s %d" % (k, new_counts[k]) for k in keys)),
               ("coverage", "none" if uncovered else "yes")]
    if args.no_write:
        print("not written (--no-write)")
    else:
        scan_dir = resolve_path(desc.scan_dir, os.getcwd())
        record = os.path.join(scan_dir, "%s-%s-%s.txt" % (name, args.version,
                                                          now.strftime("%Y-%m-%d")))
        if os.path.exists(record):
            raise Refusal("scan record %s exists; records are never overwritten" % record)
        staged = []
        for k in keys:
            path = resolve_path(desc.pins_files[k], os.getcwd())
            lines, i, m = read_pins(path, art)
            ending = lines[i][len(lines[i].rstrip("\r\n")):]
            lines[i] = "%s %s %s sha256:%s%s%s" % (m.group("kind"), m.group("name"),
                                                   resolved[k]["url"], resolved[k]["hash"],
                                                   m.group("tail"), ending)
            staged.append((path, "".join(lines)))
        os.makedirs(scan_dir, exist_ok=True)
        with open(record, "w") as fh:
            fh.write(render_artifact_record(name, header, new, cur, args.version, old_version))
        for path, text in staged:
            with open(path + ".tmp", "w") as fh:
                fh.write(text)
        for path, _text in staged:
            os.replace(path + ".tmp", path)
        print("wrote %s and %s" % (record, ", ".join(p for p, _t in staged)))

    for line in desc.checklist:
        print("checklist: %s" % line)
    if verdict == "exception":
        if uncovered:
            reason = ("trivy detected no package in the %s artifact, so there is no coverage"
                      % ", ".join(uncovered))
        elif not current:
            reason = ("%d fixable HIGH/CRITICAL findings (%d in all) in a first pin, which has no "
                      "current pin to compare" % (len(fixable(new)), len(new)))
        else:
            reason = ("%d fixable HIGH/CRITICAL findings (%d in all) against the current pin's "
                      "%d (%d in all)" % (len(fixable(new)), len(new), len(fixable(cur)), len(cur)))
        print("APPROVAL: %s %s %s: %s. This needs the operator's explicit approval."
              % (name, args.version, art["kind"], reason))
        return APPROVAL
    return OK


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- deploy

def state_dir():
    base = os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME") or os.path.expanduser("~/.local/state")
    return os.path.join(base, "image-bump")


def rolled_back_path(service):
    return os.path.join(state_dir(), "%s.rolled-back" % service)


def read_rolled_back(service):
    try:
        with open(rolled_back_path(service)) as fh:
            return set(ln.strip() for ln in fh if ln.strip())
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return set()


def record_rolled_back(service, digest):
    os.makedirs(state_dir(), exist_ok=True)
    with open(rolled_back_path(service), "a") as fh:
        fh.write(digest + "\n")


def find_record(scan_dir, digest):
    """The scan record whose `digest` header is `digest` (the newest by name), or None."""
    try:
        names = sorted(n for n in os.listdir(scan_dir) if n.endswith(".txt"))
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None
    for name in reversed(names):
        path = os.path.join(scan_dir, name)
        if read_record(path).get("digest") == digest:
            return path
    return None


def podman(*args):
    return run_cmd(["podman"] + list(args))


def vm(*args):
    """Run a command inside the podman VM."""
    return podman("machine", "ssh", *args)


def running_digest(container):
    """The digest of the image the container runs, read as the hardening check does."""
    ref = podman("inspect", container, "--format", "{{.ImageName}}").decode("utf-8").strip()
    if "@sha256:" not in ref:
        raise Refusal("container %s runs %r, which is not digest-pinned" % (container, ref))
    return ref.rsplit("@", 1)[1]


def image_exists(ref):
    try:
        return subprocess.run(["podman", "image", "exists", ref]).returncode == 0
    except OSError as exc:
        raise EnvError("cannot run podman: %s" % exc.strerror)


def check_passes(check):
    """Run one descriptor check (an argv list, no shell); a pass is exit 0."""
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(check["command"], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT)
    except OSError as exc:
        print("check %s: cannot run %s: %s" % (check["name"], check["command"][0], exc.strerror))
        return False
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        tail = proc.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()[-200:]
        print("check %s: failed (status %d) %s" % (check["name"], proc.returncode, tail))
        return False
    return True


def run_checks(checks, wait):
    """True when every check passes, trying again once a second for up to `wait` seconds."""
    deadline = time.monotonic() + wait
    while True:
        results = [check_passes(c) for c in checks]
        if all(results):
            print("checks passed: %s" % ", ".join(c["name"] for c in checks))
            return True
        if time.monotonic() >= deadline:  # guard: bounded-wait
            return False
        time.sleep(1)


def deploy_service(args, desc, directory):
    if is_artifact(desc):  # guard: no-artifact-deploy
        raise Refusal("%s is an artifact descriptor: an image build's pinned inputs are moved by "
                      "`check` and installed by the next build, so there is nothing to deploy"
                      % args.service)
    if desc.install["kind"] == "manual":  # guard: manual-install
        print("deploy: %s is installed by hand on its host; steps:" % args.service)
        for step in desc.install["steps"]:
            print("  %s" % step)
        return REFUSED
    image, tag, applied = read_pin(resolve_path(desc.pin_file, directory))
    version = version_from_tag(tag, getattr(desc, "tag_suffix", ""))
    record = find_record(resolve_path(desc.scan_dir, directory), applied)
    if record is None:  # guard: record-found
        raise Refusal("no scan record for %s in %s: run `image-bump check` first"
                      % (applied, resolve_path(desc.scan_dir, directory)))
    header = read_record(record)
    replaces, migrates = header.get("replaces", ""), header.get("migrates")
    if not replaces or migrates not in ("yes", "no"):
        raise Refusal("scan record %s lacks `replaces` or a yes/no `migrates`" % record)
    migrates = migrates == "yes"

    running = running_digest(desc.container)
    if running == applied:  # guard: applied-differs
        raise Refusal("pin not applied or already running: %s runs %s, the applied pin"
                      % (desc.container, running))
    if running != replaces:  # guard: running-is-replaces
        raise Refusal("%s runs %s, but the scan record says this version replaces %s"
                      % (desc.container, running, replaces))
    if not image_exists("%s@%s" % (image, replaces)):  # guard: image-exists
        raise Refusal("the running image %s@%s is not in the VM's storage, so a rollback would "
                      "have nothing to restore" % (image, replaces))
    if applied in read_rolled_back(args.service):  # guard: rolled-back
        raise Refusal("%s was rolled back before; revert the pin instead (%s)"
                      % (applied, rolled_back_path(args.service)))

    vm_unit, kept = desc.vm_unit, "%s.pre-%s" % (desc.vm_unit, version)
    export = os.path.expanduser("~/%s-pre-%s.tar" % (desc.volume, version))
    try:
        if migrates:  # step: export
            vm("systemctl", "--user", "stop", desc.service)
            podman("volume", "export", desc.volume, "-o", export)
            print("exported %s to %s" % (desc.volume, export))
        vm("cp", vm_unit, kept)  # step: keep
        print("kept the unit as %s" % kept)
    except EnvError as exc:
        raise EnvError("%s (nothing was installed%s)" % (exc, "; the service may be stopped" if migrates else ""))

    failed = None
    try:
        install = desc.install
        if install["kind"] == "vm-copy":  # step: install
            vm("cp", resolve_path(install["applied_unit"], directory), vm_unit)
            vm("systemctl", "--user", "daemon-reload")
            vm("systemctl", "--user", "restart", desc.service)
        else:
            sys.stdout.flush()
            rc = subprocess.run([resolve_path(install["path"], directory)]).returncode
            if rc != 0:
                failed = "the install script exited %d" % rc
    except (EnvError, OSError) as exc:
        failed = str(exc)
    if failed is None and not run_checks(desc.checks, args.wait):
        failed = "a check failed"

    if failed is None:
        print("deployed %s:%s@%s; the unit before it is kept as %s" % (image, tag, applied, kept))
        print("reminder: tell the other users of the service before and after any restart of it")
        if migrates:
            print("reminder: delete %s once the nightly backup has taken it" % export)
        return OK

    print("FAILED: %s" % failed)
    if migrates:  # guard: migrating-no-restore
        print("not restoring anything: the version migrates, and restoring is a person's call")
        print("the volume export is %s; the previous unit is %s" % (export, kept))
        return STOPPED
    back = False
    try:
        vm("cp", kept, vm_unit)
        vm("systemctl", "--user", "daemon-reload")
        vm("systemctl", "--user", "restart", desc.service)
        back = run_checks(desc.checks, args.wait)
        print("rolled back to %s; the checks %s" % (kept, "pass again" if back else "STILL FAIL"))
    except EnvError as exc:
        print("ROLLBACK FAILED: %s" % exc)
    record_rolled_back(args.service, applied)
    print("%s is listed as rolled back; the pin must be reverted by a commit" % applied)
    if not back:  # guard: not-recovered
        # Exit 5 tells the caller the service is up on the previous version; here it is not.
        print("NOT RECOVERED: the service is not passing its checks on the previous unit (%s); "
              "stopped for a person" % kept)
        return STOPPED
    return ROLLED_BACK


def with_descriptor(args, work):
    try:
        desc, directory = load_descriptor(args.root, args.service)
        problems = descriptor_problems(desc, directory)
        if problems:
            raise DescriptorError(problems)
        return work(args, desc, directory)
    except DescriptorError as exc:
        for p in exc.problems:
            sys.stderr.write("image-bump: %s: %s\n" % (args.service, p))
        return REFUSED
    except Refusal as exc:
        sys.stderr.write("image-bump: refused: %s\n" % exc)
        return REFUSED
    except EnvError as exc:
        sys.stderr.write("image-bump: environment: %s\n" % exc)
        return USAGE


def cmd_deploy(args):
    return with_descriptor(args, deploy_service)


def build_parser():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="image-bump", description="Move a service's digest-pinned image to a newer release.")
    sub = parser.add_subparsers(dest="verb", metavar="{check,deploy,latest,validate}")
    sub.required = True
    for verb, func in (("check", cmd_check), ("deploy", cmd_deploy),
                       ("latest", cmd_latest), ("validate", cmd_validate)):
        p = sub.add_parser(verb)
        p.add_argument("service")
        if verb == "check":
            p.add_argument("version")
            p.add_argument("--no-write", action="store_true",
                           help="decide and report; write neither the record nor the pin")
        if verb == "latest":
            p.add_argument("--pin", default=None, metavar="VERSION",
                           help="compare against this version instead of the pinned one")
        if verb == "deploy":
            p.add_argument("--wait", type=int, default=60,
                           help="seconds to retry the checks before declaring failure (default 60)")
        p.add_argument("--root", default=None,
                       help="directory holding the service directories (default: see --help of "
                            "the tool's header)")
        p.set_defaults(func=func)
    return parser


def main(argv=None):
    try:
        args = build_parser().parse_args(argv)
        if args.root is None:
            args.root = default_root(args.verb)
        return args.func(args)
    except SystemExit as exc:
        # argparse exits 2 on usage errors and 0 on --help; never let a 1 through.
        code = exc.code
        return code if isinstance(code, int) and code != 1 else (OK if code in (None, 0) else USAGE)
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 -- the contract is: never exit 1
        sys.stderr.write("image-bump: error: %s: %s\n" % (type(exc).__name__, exc))
        return USAGE


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
